{"id":"pr-980-draft-qedit-tx-user-controls","number":null,"title":"Transaction User Controls","status":"Draft","statusDetail":null,"category":"Consensus","owners":["Antoine Rondelet","Pablo Kogan"],"created":"2024-12-22","license":"MIT","discussionsTo":"https://forum.zcashcommunity.com/t/introducing-transaction-controls-in-zcash/49640","pullRequest":null,"kind":"proposal","format":"rst","source":"https://github.com/zcash/zips/blob/034378623ada1fa050feb0189a635d396308f453/zips/draft-qedit-tx-user-controls.rst","official":"https://github.com/zcash/zips/pull/980","updatedAt":"2025-07-08T08:52:21Z","summary":"This ZIP specifies user controls for transactions - a mechanism allowing recipients of shielded funds to actively participate in the transfer of Assets by approving or rejecting incoming transactions.","words":1661,"digest":"9db806ff5ac982cbd5f51d96e29ecfbc3bb2d0109ab8bbce7fc6ab10815d5685","headings":[{"id":"terminology","title":"Terminology","level":2},{"id":"abstract","title":"Abstract","level":2},{"id":"motivation","title":"Motivation","level":2},{"id":"overview","title":"Overview","level":2},{"id":"specification","title":"Specification","level":2},{"id":"approval-signature","title":"Approval Signature","level":3},{"id":"rationale-for-approval-signature","title":"Rationale for Approval Signature","level":4},{"id":"modifications-to-the-orchard-statement-circuit","title":"Modifications to the Orchard Statement/Circuit","level":3},{"id":"rationale-for-the-modifications-to-the-orchard-statement-circuit","title":"Rationale for the modifications to the Orchard Statement/Circuit","level":4},{"id":"modifications-to-the-transaction-format","title":"Modifications to the Transaction Format","level":3},{"id":"other-considerations","title":"Other Considerations","level":2},{"id":"transaction-fees","title":"Transaction Fees","level":3},{"id":"malicious-recipients","title":"Malicious Recipients","level":3},{"id":"references","title":"References","level":2}],"body":[{"tag":"h2","children":["Terminology"],"id":"terminology"},{"tag":"p","children":["The key word \"MUST\" in this document is to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","1","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-1","href":"#bcp14"}," when, and only when, it appears in all capitals."]},{"tag":"p","children":["The term \"network upgrade\" in this document is to be interpreted as described in ZIP 200 ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","2","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-2","href":"#zip-0200"},"."]},{"tag":"p","children":["The terms \"Orchard\" and \"Action\" in this document are to be interpreted as described in ZIP 224 ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","4","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-3","href":"#zip-0224"},"."]},{"tag":"p","children":["The terms \"Asset\" and \"ZSA\" in this document are to be interpreted as described in ZIP 227 ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","5","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-4","href":"#zip-0227"},"."]},{"tag":"h2","children":["Abstract"],"id":"abstract"},{"tag":"p","children":["This ZIP specifies user controls for transactions - a mechanism allowing recipients of shielded funds to actively participate in the transfer of Assets by approving or rejecting incoming transactions."]},{"tag":"h2","children":["Motivation"],"id":"motivation"},{"tag":"p","children":["In the current version of Zcash, fund transfers occur without the explicit consent of recipients. While this simplicity offers convenience, it creates significant challenges for users of the network (e.g. individuals or businesses). The goal of this ZIP is to design a mechanism where the recipient of shielded funds on Zcash (for any type of ZSA) can confirm (or ‘approve’) the receipt of the funds on chain. This enhancement addresses crucial needs in the Zcash ecosystem, particularly at a time when privacy-preserving assets face increased scrutiny from major cryptocurrency exchanges. The proposed controls offer robust safeguarding solutions while maintaining Zcash’s core privacy features."]},{"tag":"h2","children":["Overview"],"id":"overview"},{"tag":"p","children":["In the current Zcash network, users identify each other through addresses. When two parties want to transact, the sender must first obtain the recipient's address, typically through off-chain communications like email or forums. Recipients can control incoming payments by selectively sharing their addresses, effectively \"authorizing\" specific users to send them funds."]},{"tag":"p","children":["While some users or organizations (e.g. charities like Turing Trust) may choose to publicly share their addresses, others prefer to restrict address sharing to specific parties. However, this address-based authorization system has several fundamental limitations:"]},{"tag":"ol","children":[{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["No Revocation Mechanism: Once an address is shared, recipients cannot revoke a sender's ability to transfer funds. While generating new addresses is possible, the sender can still send funds to old addresses, potentially leading to lost funds."]}]},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Lack of Fine-grained Control: Sharing an address grants unrestricted payment authorization. Recipients cannot set conditions on incoming payments, such as specific amounts or timing restrictions (e.g., for tax purposes)."]}]},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Uncontrolled Transitivity: Address sharing is transitive - if recipient Alice shares her address with Bob, who then shares it with Charlie, Charlie gains the same payment capabilities as Bob. This means recipients lose control over who can ultimately send them funds, as their address may be shared (maliciously or through compromise) with unintended parties."]}]}]},{"tag":"p","children":["Currently, any sender with a recipient's address can unilaterally initiate transfers. This capability creates several risks:"]},{"tag":"ul","children":[{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Establishing unwanted links between recipients and a specific Asset"]}]},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Associating recipients with tainted funds (e.g. for blackmail or reputational damage)"]}]},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Creating undesired connections between recipients and other users or transactions"]}]}]},{"tag":"p","children":["This unilateral transfer capability presents practical challenges across the ecosystem. Centralized exchanges (CEXs) cannot reject fraudulent deposits, leading some to delist privacy-preserving assets like Zcash ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","7","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-5","href":"#zcash-delist"},". These delistings impact the entire Zcash community by reducing asset liquidity and accessibility."]},{"tag":"p","children":["Furthermore, since recipients are not actively involved in transfers, the sender bears sole responsibility for transaction accuracy. Common mistakes like address typos regularly result in lost funds. While traditional payment systems (e.g. for CHAPS or Faster Payments in the UK) have developed safeguards against such errors (e.g., Confirmation of Payee ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","6","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-6","href":"#confirmation-of-payee"},"), cryptocurrency transfers lack similar protections."]},{"tag":"p","children":["This ZIP introduces an interactive protocol requiring recipient approval for asset transfers. The recipient generates an approval signature for the Orchard Action and returns it to the sender, who then submits the complete transaction to the network. The network verifies the approval by checking that the signature was generated by the intended recipient."]},{"tag":"p","children":["This double opt-in protocol provides two key benefits:"]},{"tag":"ol","children":[{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Prevention of accidental fund loss (i.e. safeguarding)"]}]},{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["Recipient control over incoming transfers"]}]}]},{"tag":"h2","children":["Specification"],"id":"specification"},{"tag":"p","children":["Most of the protocol is kept the same as the Orchard protocol released with NU5, except for the following."]},{"tag":"h3","children":["Approval Signature"],"id":"approval-signature"},{"tag":"p","children":["Let ",{"tag":"math","children":[{"tag":"mo","children":["("],"attrs":{"stretchy":"false"}},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖽"]},{"tag":"mo","children":[","]},{"tag":"mrow","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗉"]},{"tag":"msub","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗄"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖽"]}]}]},{"tag":"mo","children":[")"],"attrs":{"stretchy":"false"}}]}," be the Orchard shielded payment address of the recipient of the output note of an Orchard Action ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","9","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-7","href":"#protocol-raw-address"},". Let ",{"tag":"math","children":[{"tag":"msub","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝗀"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖽"]}]}]}," be derived from the diversifier ",{"tag":"math","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝖽"]}]}," as in §5.4.1.6 of the protocol specification ",{"tag":"a","children":["[","10","]"],"id":"footnote-reference-8","href":"#protocol-diversify-hash"},". The approval signature is derived as follows:"]},{"tag":"ol","children":[{"tag":"li","children":[{"tag":"p","children":["The sender sends the ",{"tag":"math","children":[{"tag":"mrow","children":[{"tag":"mi","children":["𝙾"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚛"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚌"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚑"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚊"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚛"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚍"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝙰"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚌"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚝"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚒"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚘"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚗"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝙳"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚎"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚜"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚌"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚛"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚒"]},{"tag":"mi","children":["𝚙"]},{"tag":"